  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when a project doesn't go as planned, and garage wall framing is no exception. One common hiccup is inaccurate layout, where studs might be off-center, or openings for doors and windows just aren't quite right. This often happens because the stud centers weren't marked consistently on the plates, leading to a domino effect of issues. Another big one is not double-checking if the concrete slab is level or if anchor placements are correct before you even start framing. If the base isn't solid, your walls will end up out of plumb, and that's a headache for connections and inspections. You also really need to pay attention to headers around garage door openings. If they're undersized or framed incorrectly, you could end up with sagging or a failed inspection. And don't forget about hold-downs or anchor bolts—skipping these or not fastening plates and sheathing adequately can compromise the whole structure. Finally, a big mistake we see is people rushing to insulate or drywall before that crucial framing inspection. If you do that, and the inspector finds issues, you'll be tearing things out and starting over. Getting professional help early on, especially with HH Remodeling LLC, can prevent these kinds of costly errors and ensure your project in areas like Denver or Centennial is done right the first time. We've got the experience to spot these issues before they become major problems.",
